      "answer": {
        "coverage": "complete",
        "insufficient_evidence": false,
        "summary": "Moesta recommends a concise, Pixar-inspired sequence that traces your story from what you were like before a turning point through what changed and where that journey led. It should help the listener grasp your core skills, feel the journey, and become curious about where you want to go.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S1",
          "S2"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "Practical structure",
            "content": "Use a short, roughly seven-statement, elevator-pitch-style story. Moesta’s example follows a causal arc: “Once upon a time” (your background and what you were like), “every day” (your recurring interests or strengths), “one day” (the realization or change), and “because of that” (the consequences and steps that followed), ending with what you ultimately built or where you want to go. He says to use it to cover your core skills, why you changed, and the journey you have been through. Keep it distilled and concise.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S2"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "What the story should help the listener understand",
            "content": "The story should let the listener see the journey and feel what you are trying to do, while giving them enough of a hook to ask questions and want to understand more. In an interview, it is a way to make your career story engaging and help people get excited about hiring you.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S2"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "missing_topics": []
      },
