        "S7": {
          "chunk_id": "c6fe56dc-43b7-4713-8b0e-1ed595b44d53",
          "episode_id": "8ebf379f-f966-4ef8-94c7-b9c76186fa3d",
          "episode_revision_id": "c68562b0-a2ef-401a-a6f1-43ed7ea69d56",
          "title": "How a great founder becomes a great CEO | Jonathan Lowenhar (co-founder of Enjoy The Work)",
          "guest": "Jonathan Lowenhar",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s0jn7eE33nk",
          "archive_member": "episodes/jonathan-lowenhar/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": "ted today, 12 months have gone by, you're clinking champagne because of how great it's been. What's changed about the business? What does success look like 12 months later? Document it. And then when you interview people, look for people that have already done that stuff.\n\n(00:52:13):\nSecond, the notion of does the person in front of you have a history of creating raving fans? They talk in the book about this idea of you being pulled or pushed in your career. If you were an outstanding performer in Job A, it is a high likelihood that in job B, someone associated with you in job A is going to pull you into the next thing and then pull you into the next thing and pull you into the next thing and you'll never do a job search 'cause you were great. And if you see a history of that, ding, ding, ding, ding, ding, really attractive candidate. Third, core values matter a lot. Culture is not an accident. Culture at scale is the codification of what matters to a business and the ritualization of living those values. It starts with whoever the founders are and then it will emanate across as long as the founders are super consistent. But that also means you need a methodology for evaluating the next human in front of you on whether they actually represent your values. We think of these interview stages in a way similar to the book, we use slightly different language, but we think that there is a culture interview, there is a functional interview, and there is a technical interview, but they're designed to get at these notions of have they actually done this kind of work before? Have they been pulled or pushed in their career and are they your kind of human?\n\n",
          "start_char": 52568,
          "end_char": 54243,
          "start_seconds": 3078,
          "end_seconds": 3133,
          "similarity": 0.5703752060403896
        },
        "S8": {
          "chunk_id": "6e76e909-5fca-4eb4-97c2-217645fc3a10",
          "episode_id": "b18777e7-3aa3-4112-bd38-1aefd62f1170",
          "episode_revision_id": "e24e9cd9-d279-4072-b720-3614f0979fa4",
          "title": "Billion dollar failures, and billion dollar success | Tom Conrad (Quibi, Pandora, Pets.com, Zero)",
          "guest": "Tom Conrad",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=maK0XD9ARoI",
          "archive_member": "episodes/tom-conrad/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": "erview. So I tell people that almost every interview question should start with, \"Tell me about a time in your career when...\" to give them permission or to set their expectation that I'm asking them to tell me about something they've actually done that is relevant to the topic that I'm probing.\n\n(01:33:59):\nSo that's just a structural thing that I try to do. And then usually towards the end of the interview, I ask almost everybody, \"Imagine that you had a really great day at work. What was it that you did on that day?\" Because what I'm trying to figure out is left to their own devices, what do they go to naturally because it rewards them? Because I think it's really important to find the highest best use of everyone you hire. And sometimes you can get a little bit to the bottom of what's the highest best use if you understand the things where there's a natural reward mechanism because it's like, \"Well, I had this great day. I'm going to do more of that tomorrow.\" \"What was the thing?\" And it's just pretty illuminating.\n\nLenny (01:34:45):\nIs there a favorite product you've recently discovered that you really love?\n\n",
          "start_char": 89199,
          "end_char": 90332,
          "start_seconds": 5579,
          "end_seconds": 5685,
          "similarity": 0.5535152777790512
        },
        "S9": {
          "chunk_id": "985a9058-bb04-48a6-b786-654a767110a4",
          "episode_id": "d4205a12-a48d-4798-8717-67bbe00efc4a",
          "episode_revision_id": "79dfaeb6-b391-439e-90da-2bca11545664",
          "title": "How to discover your superpowers, own your story, and unlock personal growth | Donna Lichaw",
          "guest": "Donna Lichaw",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7fA8DMMUNPI",
          "archive_member": "episodes/donna-lichaw/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": "ly step up and lead. I didn't have an answer for this team at the time, and I left this offsite just honestly feeling terrible and not knowing what to do about it except telling them, \"I think you got to go work with a coach because this is beyond my pay grade.\"\n\n(00:08:05):\nBut when I left that day, I just couldn't stop thinking about this team because I had a hunch that stories were still a part of something but not what they needed. Telling stories was not the answer, and so I ended up spending what now is the next decade figuring out, all right, how do you become an effective leader? And of course because I can't do anything lightly, once I went down that path, I ended up switching up my entire business. And now that's all I do is help people be better, more effective, confident leaders who really make the impact that they want to make.\n\nLenny (00:08:39):\nAlong those same lines, what's interesting is you actually found that this idea of storytelling was actually much more effective in your coaching practice. Instead of helping them figure out the story of the product, it's the story of the person. And so transitioning into the meat of your approach to coaching, I think you call it story-driven leadership. Essentially, your finding is that story and the person's story is extremely important and powerful in helping them level up as a leader and also seeing them as the hero of their story. I guess just with that foundation, can you just talk about why that is so powerful, the idea of figuring out your own story and why you need to be the hero of your own story and just what all this means?\n\n",
          "start_char": 7865,
          "end_char": 9484,
          "start_seconds": 416,
          "end_seconds": 519,
          "similarity": 0.5308237584429093
        },
        "S10": {
          "chunk_id": "2a426b33-af7c-47e5-8017-77def5e725e4",
          "episode_id": "445c48da-f272-4e3f-b78f-4729d35aeab5",
          "episode_revision_id": "8b690be1-2f31-467c-b64a-06224422b0c1",
          "title": "How to tell better stories | Matthew Dicks (Storyworthy)",
          "guest": "Matthew Dicks",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J4wguyJZI6A",
          "archive_member": "episodes/matthew-dicks/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": " I thought it'd be fun to start with maybe the most mind-expanding takeaway I got from this book, is this idea that all good stories are rooted in this five second moment of someone's life. Can you just talk about this insight and maybe share an example or two to make this real?\n\nMatthew Dicks (00:05:28):\nSure. Well, that is true what you just said, which is essentially every story is about a singular moment. I call it five seconds. It can be one second honestly. It's a moment of either transformation, meaning I'm telling you a story about how I once used to be one kind of person and now I'm a new kind of person. Or more common is realization. Which is I used to think something and then some stuff happened and now I think a new thing. And those changes they take place overtime. Or really what happens is it's an accumulation of events and feelings and thoughts that ultimately result in a singular moment where that flip actually happens. And I think that's true for almost everyone. It feels like it took a long time, but there really was one second when you thought one thing and then the next second when you thought the new thing.\n\n(00:06:19):\nAnd the purpose of a story is essentially to bring that moment to the greatest clarity possible to the audience. So that the audience can, in a way experience that flip that transformation or realization along with the storyteller. So 98% of the story is the context to bring that singular moment into fruition. And that is true for stories that we tell out loud, stories we tell on the page, novels that I write, movies that I watch, television shows that I watch, all of the stories of the world that are worth hearing and truly just about every story told that qualifies as a story has one of those moments.\n\n",
          "start_char": 5660,
          "end_char": 7431,
          "start_seconds": 312,
          "end_seconds": 379,
          "similarity": 0.44289744316976853
        },
        "S11": {
          "chunk_id": "4c574c19-c704-41ff-be5e-714cb355a937",
          "episode_id": "2db1c1f8-7444-43dc-9f48-baf952946b8c",
          "episode_revision_id": "088d4c2c-4b14-4de3-9cc5-dff99cb4bdd4",
          "title": "Storytelling with Nancy Duarte: How to craft compelling presentations and tell a story that sticks",
          "guest": "Nancy Duarte",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-kHkWgjGD7U",
          "archive_member": "episodes/nancy-duarte/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": " you're trying to do that, what are signs that you're doing it well or not well? Is there the way the flow of the story start? Is it the here's the way it starts? Or what should people identify of I'm doing this well or I'm not doing this well?\n\nNancy Duarte (00:23:31):\nIf the audience is the hero, you would see visible signs that they get it. People would come before I did a really good talk and people were tweeting saying, \"Hey, come to this talk. It's really good.\" So, you'd see a reaction. You know you've done it well if you're infusing your talk with story, which is the second bullet by utilizing story structures. So, when I say storytelling, I'm talking about an anecdote. When I say story structures, I'm talking about this format of a three act structure of storytelling that goes back tens of thousands of years, which is fused into the brain like FMRI machines now you can see them while a story's being told and the science is beautiful, if you're telling me a story and I'm listening, our brains are firing in the exact same order, in the exact same place. So, it has power to align our brains.\n\n(00:24:26):\nAnd so by implementing attributes of story like a beginning, a middle, and an end, and we have method for that. And in also incorporating the rise and fall story kind of builds tension and releases it. And that's why we love it so much is we escape through someone else's messy middle and conflict and problems like it's messy and then it resolves. You build the tension and resolve it. And that's what a really well structured presentation can do. It can pull on that rise and fall in a way that creates longing.\n\n",
          "start_char": 24851,
          "end_char": 26494,
          "start_seconds": 1390,
          "end_seconds": 1466,
          "similarity": 0.5381164853432373
        }
      },
      "used_context_tokens": 3886,
      "context_hit": true,
      "answer_seconds": 5.186997400131077,
      "answer": {
        "coverage": "complete",
        "insufficient_evidence": false,
        "summary": "Bob Moesta recommends a concise, Pixar-inspired sequence—“once upon a time,” “every day,” “one day,” and the chain of what happened because of that—to tell a career journey. It should help the listener understand your core skills, what changed, how you got to where you are, and where you want to go.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S1",
          "S2"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "Practical structure",
            "content": "Use a short, roughly elevator-pitch sequence of statements: “Once upon a time…” to set up who you were; “Every day…” to show your recurring interests or core skills; “One day…” to describe the change or realization; then use “because of that…” to connect the consequences and the journey that followed. Moesta says the approach comes from Pixar and describes it as a way to distill the story rather than give a long account.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S2"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "What the story should help the listener understand",
            "content": "The story should make your core skills, the reason you changed, and the journey you have been through—and where you want to go—clear. Keep it concise enough to intrigue the listener and invite questions, while helping them see and feel the journey and giving you a high-level vision of your direction.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "missing_topics": []
      },
